Background and history                                          Page 6


Welcome to Sorcerer's Bane.  Sorcerer's Bane is a dungeon game of
mystery and intrigue where a character which you define runs through the
Dungeon of Mogadore to face the evil Lodi himself at the bottom of the
dungeon. (Hmmm. I wonder if you're up to it.)

The town of Mogadore was supposed to be the site of a magician's academy of
mammoth proportions.  Two powerful wizards, Lodi and Sabee, were dedicated to
the development of this learning institution.  These wizards developed two
STAFFS OF POWER to aid them in both the development of the academy and for
protection while they were digging through the dungeons of Mogadore.
                               
Ah, but the best made plans often go awry.  For some 'unknown reason', Lodi
turned evil.  Lodi killed Sabee by taking him by surprise, in the hopes of
combining the near-omnipotent power of the two staffs. (GASP! This is more
thrilling than a Dudley Doright rerun.)

For some OTHER 'unknown reason', Lodi was not able to manipulate Sabee's staff.
Lodi could have destroyed it, I suppose, but with that much magic being
released, Lodi's very existence would be threatened.  He opted for the safe
route, and divided the staff (carefully!) into four quarterstaffs.  He then
tricked, paid, or forced four dragons to guard the four quarterstaffs.

Lodi then barred himself at the bottom of his newly-created dungeon to go over
plans for the evil domination of the entire realm!  He gathered every type of
evil, nasty, and cruel monster to populate the dungeon to stop would-be
adventurers (like yourself) from thwarting his plans.

Your mission, should you choose to acce . . .(Oops. Wrong script!)  Ahem!  What
you need to do is fight your way to the bottom of this dungeon, recover the
parts of the other staff, save the girl, (Oops. No girl. Scratch that last one.)
answer the 'unknown reasons', and save the realm.  (And YOU thought this would
be difficult.)

Don't descend into the dungeon too quickly.  The monsters get harder to fight
as you descend, and your character must have both the experience and the magic
items needed to fight them.

You will find magic items and gold in the dungeon left by now-dead adventurers.
Magic items need to be either worn or used.  Magic items will not affect your
character until they are implemented.  Not all magic items are good, so be
careful.


Background and history (continued)                              Page 7

Above the dungeon is the town.  You may want to visit some of the stores in
town before going too deep into the dungeon.  In town, you can buy magic items,
get healed, have cursed items removed, identify items, and much more.  However,
all this takes money, so be sure to find some money in the dungeon first.

So create a new character and start adventuring.  Be careful, fight smart and
well, and you may just survive to tell your tale to others.  Oh yes, and don't
forget to print the registration, fill it out, and register.  Good luck, and
Godspeed!

SB system requirements                                          Page 8


SB requires about 700KB of disk space to run.  The actual requirements would
be 500KB to run plus about 65KB per character running. 700KB would give
you 3 characters.  If you are administrating this, you should keep these
numbers in mind.  If several people are accessing this at once or if one
person is creating many characters, it will use precious disk space.

SB requires at least 340KB of accessible RAM.  This is enough for most
users except for the original PCs, which only had 256KB.  Sorry.  I tried
to fit it in that space, but it came down to either don't make it as
fully featured or scale up the memory requirements.

SB runs much better on a hard drive or a RAMDRIVE than on a floppy, although
running from a floppy is certainly possible.

SB can use a mouse, but by no means is this necessary.  (Indeed, it was almost
an afterthought to put mouse control in.)

SB looks a lot better in color, but can be set for monochrome.  You early LCD
laptop users will want to set monochrome on, as well as you Hercules 
monochrome card owners.  SB was written, of course, to please as many people
as possible without leaving non-cutting-edge users behind.  Hopefully I
have done that.

Details on setting up SB immediately follow these requirements.


Starting SB up                                                  Page 9

SB can be run on a hard drive, floppy, or RAMDRIVE.  The following pages
describe the different ways SB can be set up on different hardware.

Also, since different users have different needs, SB is networkable.  
SB can be configured as either a single user or multi-user system.

Finally, several of the options in SB can be eliminated, such as color,
music, error beeps, and mouse control.  You will enter this screen the
first time you play SB, so be prepared.


Starting SB up - Running SB on your hard disk                   Page 10


The SB system should be in its own subdirectory.  (You'll be glad you did
if you want to copy it to someone else or (SOB!) eventually delete it off
your hard drive.)  To do this, (assuming you are loading from floppy drive
A:,) type in the following commands:
 
      C:
      CD\
      MD \SB
      CD \SB
      COPY A:*.*

Then, to run SB, simply type in the commands:
    
    CD\SB
    SB

You will need at least 700KB free, or maybe more if you run with a lot of
characters and/or a lot of players.  There now.  That wasn't so hard, was it?  


Starting SB up - Running SB from two floppy drives.            Page 11


You can also run SB from a floppy, but I wouldn't since RUNNING SB FROM A 
FLOPPY IS REEEAAAL SLOW!  This is due to the amount of disk I-O performed by 
SB.  Still, if it's all you got (assuming you have both an A: and B: drive),
do the following steps.
    
    1)  MAKE A BACKUP FIRST of your floppy!!!  Make your backup with the 
        following DOS command:

            DISKCOPY A: B:

    2)  Put your original SB disk in a safe place.

    3)  Put your copy of SB in drive B: and a COMPLETELY blank floppy in
        A:.  This is your DATA DISK.  Issue the following commands:

            COPY B:*.DAT
            COPY B:*.DUN
            COPY B:*.INV
            COPY B:*.SB*  (This one may fail.  That's OK.)

    4)  Every time you run SB, stick your DATA DISK in drive A: and your
        copy of SB in drive B:.  Type in the following commands to run
        SB from your floppies:

            A:
            B:SB

    5)  Be careful not to run this with too many live characters or on
        multi-user as your disk space will be limited.


Starting SB up - Performance considerations                   Page 12


This next section is for the somewhat technically oriented.  If that's not
you, probably just skip this or have someone explain it to you.  

To REALLY SPEED UP your SB, try running it from a RAMDRIVE.  You can set
one up if you have DOS 3.1 (I think) or above.  See your DOS manual about
putting RAMDRIVE in your CONFIG.SYS.  Always use extended or expanded memory
if available and remember that SB takes up about 340KB to run.

To run from a RAMDISK, set up the following batch (.BAT) file in your
path (ASSUMING YOUR RAMDISK IS D:.  IF IT ISN'T, CHANGE ALL OCCURRENCES
OF D: BELOW TO YOUR RAMDRIVE DRIVE)  Lower case instructions are just
comments, and please don't type them in.

      ECHO OFF           (Turn off display.  This is optional.)
      C:                 (Attach to the C drive.)
      CD\SRC\SB          (Attach to the SB source directory.)
      D:                 (Attach to the RAMDRIVE.)
      COPY C:*.INV        
      COPY C:*.DAT       (Copy all data files to the RAMDRIVE.)
      COPY C:*.SB*        
      C:SB               (Execute the SB program on the C: drive.)
      C:                 (When SB is done, attach to the C drive.)
      DEL *.DUN          (Delete old, outdated files.)
      DEL *.INV           
      COPY D:*.*         (Copy new, updated files.)
      ECHO ON            (Turn on display.  This is optional.)
 
Be careful not to run to many characters on your RAMDRIVE if space is limited.
 
I have noticed that a real good caching system (like SMARTDRV from WINDOWS 3.1
or PC-CACHE from PCTOOLS) can come REAL CLOSE to REMDRIVE performance, and a
caching system would improve your entire system performance.  DOS 4.0 and
above also comes with a version of SMARTDRV (but it's not quite up to WINDOWS).  
I HIGHLY recommend a caching not only for SB but for all applications.  Check
your DOS manual for details.


Starting SB up - Performance considerations (continued)        Page 13


Executing FASTOPEN in your AUTOEXEC.BAT for MS-DOS versions 3.3 and above
(perhaps 3.1.  I'm not sure.) will also speed up ALL repeated file access.
Some other DOS applications seem a little touchy with FASTOPEN, but I
don't boot up without it, and I'm still running.  Just be careful to remove
it when running some low-level programs like PC-TOOLS COMPRESS or NORTON
UTILITIES SPEEDDISK.  (Low-level programs are the hard ones to write, in
case you're curious.)  Check your DOS manual to see if you can run FASTOPEN.

Some programs (like WINDOWS) can speed your keyboard up.  This might make
SB more enjoyable.  There are some available on shareware. Check into it
if you're interested.

Starting SB up - Choosing the type of user you are.             Page 14


If you are the first one to run SB on this system, you will have to decide how 
you want SB administered.

You have your choice of whether to set up SB as multi-user or single-user.
The following is a guide as to which setup is best for you.

A single-user setup is when you are not running over a network and you either
are the only one who accesses this game OR you trust the other computer users
enough to not worry about password protection.  I would imagine that most of
you would pick single-user so as to not mess with passwords and the like.

A multi-user setup would configure the game to accept several users at one 
time.  This configuration would also be chosen if you several people had 
access to this computer AND you did not want them using your characters.  In 
the multi-user setup, password protection is enforced.


Starting SB up - Multi-user setup.                              Page 15


If you are the first person to use this system, you are now the defacto SB
ADMINISTRATOR.  This will give you powers and abilities beyond those of mortal
players.

Remember, this is your system, so answer the following questions carefully. If
you aren't careful, SB may take up a large chunk of your resources or too much
of your prime system hours.  Fortunately for you, SB has been created to allow
the SB ADMINISTRATOR to limit SB usage on multi-user systems.  On the following
screen, you will be asked questions about configuring the SB system.

If you have elected the multi-user setup, SB will let you choose several
options to control the use of SB on your network or PC.  The following fields
can be entered on the multi-user screen.  If you do not wish to enact any or 
all of these features, simply leave the field blank.

MAXIMUM USERS ALLOWED
This is the number of players allowed to use SB at one time.

MAX (LIVE) CHARACTERS
This is the maximum amount of characters any player can
have running concurrently in SB.  Only live characters count.

STARTING BLOCKED HOUR
Sometimes a network will be overloaded at specific times during the day
so that playing a game would be counter-productive.  (Imagine that!)  Hence, 
SB allows the SBA to declare a 'STARTING BLOCK HOUR' (or SBH).  The SBH is the
first hour in a block of hours that SB WILL NOT be allowed to be played.  For
instance, if you wanted to block SB from 8:00 A.M. to 5:00 P.M., your SBH 
would be 8.  If you don't want to limit when SB can be played at all, simply 
return through the next question.

HOURS BLOCKED
HOURS BLOCKED can disallow access to SB during certain time periods.  This 
field tells how many hours you want blocked out.

MAX MINUTES OF PLAY
This field is used to relegate the amount of contiguous time allowed on playing
SB.  Again, it is typically for company installations, etc.

Starting SB up - Multi-user setup (continued)                  Page 16

SB SYSTEM PASSWORD
SB allows you, as the system administrator, to configure a system password.
This would allow you to run ANY PLAYER's characters.  This would be useful
for answering any questions a user might have OR defining and/or reporting
any problems that may occur with the SB.

However, a SB password is a two-edged sword.  If you let your SB password
get out, it may be possible for any user to reak havok on SB.  This may cause
more trouble than it's worth.

Often, a SB ADMINISTRATOR will need to enter into a character's area to resolve
some conflict or to (hopefully not!!) recreate some bug to be reported.  This
password allows the SB ADMINISTRATOR to access any user/player running SB.

 

Starting SB up - Default screen                               Page 17

This screen is called when any new player or single user is set up in SB.
The following options let you choose the system defaults used in Sorcerer's
Bane:

(C) TURN THE COLOR ON/OFF
This allows you to toggle your game to color or monochrome.  Laptop LCD users
will probably want the color turned off.
 
(S) TURN THE SOUND (MUSIC) ON/OFF
Hey, I know that it's a great theme song and you could listen to it for hours,
(I like to call it "Bastardized Beethoven"), but should you be so inclined,
you could toggle the music off and on with this option.

(E) TURN THE ERROR ALARM ON/OFF
Those beeps can get annoying, especially late at night or on the job.  This
option turns off the error beep.

(M) TURN THE MOUSE ON/OFF
This game works fine with a numeric keypad, or even a 4-key arrow keypad.  It
also works with a mouse.  You can turn this option off, especially if you are
running this in OS/2 2.0 partial screen. (This hurts my mouse control.)  This
option will not display if you do not have a recognizable mouse.

(D) TURN THE MOUSE BUTTON DESCRIPTION ON/OFF
In SB, the two mouse buttons usually stay pretty consistent.  The left button
means ENTER (choose, etc.).  The right button means ESC (Quit, Run, No, etc.).
The actual meaning can be displayed if this option is on.

(H) HELP
Help displays this text.
 
(Q OR ESC) SAVE & GO TO MAIN OR PREVIOUS MENU
This option always saves the default information.  It then usually goes back
to the previous calling menu UNLESS it is on a new player or when Single user
is defined.  It will then go to the MAIN MENU.


Creating a character                                            Page 18

Before you can have any fun in SB, you must first create a character.  This
is done through with the (C) option on the main menu.  Creating a character
is easy, and can be done with minimal time or effort.  The following pages
will step you through the character creation process.


Creating a character - Rolling statistics                    Page 19

The six main statistics, along with random events, determine how well your
character will perform in Sorcerer's Bane.  The following is a description
of each one and how each one affects you.  Unlike typical adventure games,
each statistic will affect the majority of events in Sorcerer's Bane.  (i.e.
a smart fighter will fight better than a stupid one.)

STR
Strength - Strength describes how physically strong your character is. It
           primarily determines how much damage you do once you hit an
           opponent, but also affects your hitting ability, your protection
           protection and your ability to talk to and purchase from the shop.

INT
Intelligence - Intelligence mainly affects your ability to use magic,
               magical items, and resist magic, and your alertness.  It
               also affects your hitting ability, your protection, your
               avoiding ability, your ability to run away, and your charm.

CON
Constitution - Constitution is how well you take a hit.  It determines how
               well you can take a punch, along with how many hit points
               you receive each time you go up a level.

DEX
Dexterity - Dexterity is how agile you are.  It mainly affects your
            ability to hit an opponent, your protection, your ability
            to avoid being hit, and your ability to run away.  It also
            affects your magical resistance, your avoiding ability, your
            ability to use a magical item, and your alertness.

CHR
Charisma - Charisma is how good you look.  It is the major component of
           your charm, which is used when talking to an opponent or buying
           from the shop.

LCK
LUCK - Luck affects EVERY extended attribute, but only affects them minimally.

Creating a character - Choosing a race                          Page 20


Your race should be chosen to reflect what kind of character you wish to be.
All races except Human affect your statistics, either raising them or lowering
them as that race relates to the Human race in Sorcerer's Bane.  Please note
that some of the description might not match up to other adventure games, but
for various reasons, these are the one's used in Sorcerer's Bane.

HUMAN
A human has the traits that all other races are measured by in
Sorcerer's Bane.  As the second strongest race, Humans make good
fighters, but can excel in any class.

TROLL
A Troll is a creature from the bitter Coldlands. Surviving in such
a harsh environment has made Trolls a very hardy and strong race,
while at the same time not doing much for their appearance.  Trolls
rely on strength, hardiness, and agility to survive.  They are not
great thinkers.  Like the elves, Trolls scorn luck as unreliable,
preferring to take matters into their own hands.

ELF
An Elf is a creature from the woodlands.  They are considered beautiful
and magical.  Though not as hardy or strong as a Human, they usually
are more intelligent and more nimble.  Elves tend to scorn luck as
unreliable.  Elves make good magicians.

DWARF
Dwarves usually live underground.  They are famous for their work with
metal and mining.  They tend to be somewhat smaller than Humans.  This
allows them to fit into smaller places, like the mines.  They are not as
strong as Humans due to their size, but they make up for it with their
hardiness and agility.  Dwarves tend to be more intelligent than Humans
due to much self-reflecting. Like the Elves and the Trolls, Dwarves
scorn luck as a weakling's way out. Dwarves can excel in any class.
                      
GNOME
Gnomes share the woodland with the Elves.  They are not as beautiful as
the Humans and the Elves, due to their short and pudgy stature.  Gnomes
live in trees and are very close to nature.  They are the weakest of all
the classes.  They tend to live in milder climates, and are not too
hardy. Unlike the Elves, Trolls, and Dwarves, Gnomes view luck as
desirable and someone who is lucky as close to nature.  They are nimble
and have an enlightened outlook on life.  They make very good magicians.


Creating a character - Choosing a class                         Page 21


Your class tells what kind of character you are and what powers you have.  The
brief description following will describe each class:

FIGHTER
A fighter is good at fighting.  He does not cast spells or sing songs
like a mage or a bard, but he can use magical items.  A fighter should
have good strength and dexterity.  Humans and Trolls make good
fighters.

MAGE
A mage, or magician, is the spell-caster.  He can fight, especially
when he runs out of spells, but spells are what he usually uses
if possible.  A mage should have high intelligence.  Elves, Dwarves,
and Gnomes make good mages.

BARD
A bard fights well, but not as good as a fighter.  He sings songs
that aid in fighting or healing.  Songs are different in nature from
spells.  Songs usually work slower but are more constant than spells.
A bard also never runs out of songs once he learns them.  However, his
songs are every bit as powerful as spells, but are fewer in number.
Any class can make a good bard.

MAIN MENU                                                       Page 22

The MAIN MENU pops up first every time you run SB (with the exception of the
install, which only pops up once).  This menu allows you to inquire about
certain game facts or to start running either a new or existing character in
SB.  The options for this menu are as follows:

(C) CREATE A NEW CHARACTER
This option lets you design a character suitable for running in
Sorcerer's Bane.  You will be able to choose a character's main statistics,
race, and class.  You must create a character before using one.

(U) USE AN EXISTING CHARACTER
This option lets you use a previously created character in Sorcerer's Bane.
The character you pick can visit the cleric or the shop or run in the dungeon

(C) LIST ALL CHARACTERS
This option lists all the characters currently running in Sorcerer's Bane.

(A) SHOW A CHARACTER'S ATTRIBUTES
This option shows the attributes of an existing character.

(F) FIND THE EXPERIENCE FOR A LEVEL
This option allows you to find the experience level needed to reach different
levels for different classes.

(K) KILL AN EXISTING CHARACTER
This option 'kills' an existing character and releases any disk space this
character may be using.

(D) CHANGE SYSTEM DEFAULTS
This option allows you to change certain defaults in Sorcerer's Bane like sound
and graphics.

(I) INTRODUCTION
This option reprints the introduction.  A MUST for new users.  This will answer
many questions you have about the game.

(P) PRINT THE REGISTRATION FORM
This option prints the registration form so you can become a registered user.

(H) HELP
Help prints this summary on your screen

(Q or ESC) QUIT THE GAME
As stated, this option quits the game and returns you to DOS

Character menu                                                  Page 23


(R) RUN YOUR CHOSEN CHARACTER
This option lets you run a previously created character in the dungeon.  Only
by running a character initially can you gain gold and experience.

(A) SHOW A CHARACTER'S ATTRIBUTES
This option shows the attributes of an existing character.

(S) HAVE A CHARACTER VISIT THE SHOP
This option lets the character visit the shop.  Although you can visit the shop
with no money, it won't help you.  To get gold, run in the dungeon.

(C) HAVE A CHARACTER VISIT THE CLERIC
This option lets the character visit the cleric.  The cleric can heal you and
remove cursed items and sickness that you may have acquired in the dungeon.
To get gold, run in the dungeon.

(W) MAKE A WISH
This option allows a character to make a wish.  A character must be granted
a wish either in the dungeon or by a magical item before he can make a wish.
